## Problems I'm having

1. Right now, since deploying Pi-hole and WireGuard, I wanted to see if my iPhone will be going through Pi-hole for ads to be blocked.
I went on my iPhone, went to a YouTube video, and an ad popped up. Then I went to Pi-hole to see if my device was shown in the query log. I looked for the exact domain, but it seems like it never went through.
My iPhone DNS requests aren't reaching Pi-hole at all. 

# Steps taken 

1. Noticed Pi-hole seemed to only be blocking ads on my PC, not my other devices, so I went looking into why.
2. Checked Pi-hole's Query Log to see if my other devices were even showing up in there at all.
3. Found something strange: every device was appearing under one single IP address instead of showing up separately.
4. Learned that's actually normal. My router relays DNS requests to Pi-hole using its own IP address, so Pi-hole can't tell devices apart by IP alone.
5. Tested it directly on my iPhone, loaded a page, then checked the query log for that domain. Nothing showed up, so my iPhone's DNS requests weren't reaching Pi-hole.
6. Thought about resetting the router but held off, since a factory reset would've wiped the DNS settings I already configured.
7. Realized I never actually rebooted the router since I first set up Pi-hole, so I did a normal reboot (not a factory reset) to force every device to grab fresh settings.
8. Checked iCloud Private Relay on my phone; it was already off.
9. Checked the WiFi DNS setting on my phone; it was set to Automatic.
10. Looked closer at what Automatic was actually using and found two DNS addresses listed, one starting with 2---- and one that was 1-----.
11. Figured out the 2--- one was an IPv6 address handed out separately by the router, and my iPhone was choosing that over the IPv4 address pointed at Pi-hole, which is why it was skipping Pi-hole completely.
12. I asked what DuckDNS does and learned it's not a static IP; it's Dynamic DNS, which keeps a fixed hostname pointing to my house even though my actual IP address changes.
13. Moved on to the next thing on my list, VLANs for my IoT devices. Found out my router doesn't actually support real VLANs.
14. Found a built-in IoT Network option on the router instead, made specifically for isolating smart devices. It was disabled.
15. Learned how to check which device is running DHCP on my network, using ipconfig /all on my PC or checking the router's LAN/DHCP settings page.
Confirmed I had already pointed my router's DNS at the Pi a while back, when I first set this whole thing up.
16. Went into Network Connections on the router and found the WAN IPv4 DNS Address field, confirmed the Pi's IP was in there.
17. Saw a checkbox for Override Subnet Mask and left it unchecked, since it had nothing to do with what I was fixing.
18. Saved everything and tested to make sure my devices are showing up properly in Pi-hole.
